  "notes": "Source: official Providence College 'Computer Science B.S. - Class of 2029' Academic Planning Form published by PC's Office of Academic Advising (http://academic-advising.providence.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/83/2024/11/rba11-CSC-Academic-Guidebook-1.pdf). Transcribed course-by-course from the form's official 8-semester term-by-term grids via pdftotext -layout with manual verification against the printed totals. Class of 2029 enters Fall 2025, so the form reflects the 2024-2026 Undergraduate Catalog (catalog page: https://catalog.providence.edu/preview_program.php?catoid=38&poid=1838, 'Program: Computer Science'). True degree name per the form: 'Computer Science B.S.' (Bachelor of Science in Computer Science), housed in the Department of Mathematics and Computer Science. Total: the form prints TOTAL PROGRAM OF STUDY CREDITS 125-127 because three MTH slots have 3-or-4-credit options (MTH 109 vs 131; MTH 110 vs 132; MTH elective 215+). Transcribed at the 3-credit options (MTH 109, MTH 110, 3-credit math elective), giving term totals 15+14+18+18+15+15+15+15 = 125 exactly, matching the low end of the printed 125-127 range; students choosing MTH 131/132 or a 4-credit math elective land at 126-127. Per Clarkson/UVM precedent, total_credits=125 is the sequence's own published total, not the catalog's 120-credit degree minimum (the form itself notes 'Students at Providence College must complete 120 credits'). CSC electives check out against the major-requirements list: Systems x1 (CSC SYS-1), Applications x1 (CSC APP-1), Topics x3 (CSC TOP-1/2/3). Prereqs: the planning form publishes no course prerequisites; the one officially verified prereq (PC 2024-2026 catalog Addendum, approved March 24 2025, https://catalog.providence.edu/content.php?catoid=38&navoid=1472) is that MTH 117 Discrete Mathematics and MTH 290 Foundations of Higher Mathematics are prerequisites for CSC 244 Theory of Computation — encoded as prereq ['MTH 11X'] pointing at the MTH 117-or-290 choice slot per the validator's choice-slot rule. All other prereq arrays are intentionally empty rather than invented. Form typo 'Algorithims' corrected to 'Algorithms'. Graduation notes from the form: 2.0 minimum GPA to graduate; CSC majors fulfill the Quantitative Reasoning core via MTH 109/131.",
